*.zip
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.pytest_cache/
*.egg-info/

# LLM response cache (machine-local, regenerable)
llm_prior_cache/

# Superseded/stale outputs, kept on disk but not in git
archive/
.env

# Anchor datasets: raw data + third-party clones stay out of git (hard rule).
# The compiled envelope.yaml and hand-mapping tables under src/dynbelief/anchors/
# ARE committed (small, reviewable, cited).
data/anchors/
third_party/
# HOMER: the raw upstream dataset (12 GB) as downloaded. Same hard rule —
# the loader in src/homer/ and the small derived traces under
# data/homer_traces/ ARE tracked and rebuild from a re-downloaded copy.
HOMER/

# Profile-generated symbolic episode banks (regenerable from profiles+seed;
# the hash manifest in the bank dir pins provenance).
banks/
# IPUMS ATUS microdata: redistribution is restricted by the IPUMS terms of
# use, so the extract stays local. atus/*.py (the reader/plotter) IS tracked
# and regenerates everything from a re-downloaded extract.
atus/*.dat.gz
atus/*.dat
atus/*.xml
atus/*.cbk
# Large regenerable run artifacts: a 21-day grid's run log is ~24 MB and a
# fresh one lands on every rerun. The summaries beside them (aggregate.csv,
# provenance.json, plots, healthcheck reports) stay tracked, and
# `python -m baselines.cli run <config>` rebuilds the rest.
smoke_results/**/run_log.jsonl
smoke_results/**/questions.csv

# Regenerable per-profile anchor reports (validate_profile.py output).
profiles/manual/*.anchor_report.md
results/raw_results.csv

# The budgeted-sweep raw results: 63 MB, regenerable in ~20 s by
# `python -m beliefsim.run`. The gzipped copy beside it IS tracked.

# Re-realized household timelines under extra simulator seeds. Seed 0 is
# the canonical realization and stays tracked; seeds 1+ are byte-
# deterministic from the tracked program.yaml plus the seed (no LLM) and
# ~40 MB apiece across the set, so `python -m households.reseed --seeds
# 1 2 3 4 --spatialize` rebuilds them exactly instead of git carrying
# them. Their fleet/bake-off reports under reports/baselines/*_seed<N>
# ARE tracked — those are the results, the timelines are the inputs.
profiles/households/generated/*/*/timeline_seed[1-9]*/

# Per-row dumps from the household analysis: one row per question per
# model (absence_signal, ~1.8 M rows / 33 MB) and one per filter reset
# (perpetua_resets, ~230 k rows). The AGGREGATES beside them stay tracked
# — cells.csv.gz, perpetua_edges.csv.gz, perpetua_fallback.csv.gz,
# households.json, provenance.json, the plots and the reports — and those
# are what every table and figure is built from. To rebuild the dumps:
# `python -m baselines.household_analysis` (~24 min on 100 workers), then
# `python -m baselines.perpetua_cases` for perpetua_cases.md, which reads
# absence_signal.csv.gz.
reports/baselines/**/absence_signal.csv.gz
reports/baselines/**/perpetua_resets.csv.gz

# Rate-sweep per-rate figures: four copies of the household report's
# plots, ~3 MB a rate, rebuilt in seconds from the tracked cells.csv.gz by
# `python -m baselines.household_report --in-dir reports/baselines/rate_sweep/visits<v>`.
# The cross-rate summary, its figures, findings.md and each rate's
# markdown, cells and small aggregates stay tracked.
reports/baselines/rate_sweep/visits*/*.png
# LLM belief floor: the sampled prompts / questions / completions are
# regenerable (python -m baselines.llm_floor --stage sample prompts generate)
# and prompts.jsonl alone is ~125 MB; only the summaries beside them are
# tracked.
reports/baselines/llm_floor/prompts.jsonl
reports/baselines/llm_floor/questions.jsonl
reports/baselines/llm_floor/completions.jsonl
reports/baselines/llm_floor/sample.json
reports/baselines/llm_floor/*.log

# Conformal sweep: the per-question dumps (~130 MB a run, one
# jsonl.gz per belief x policy) and run logs are regenerable by
# `python -m baselines.conformal.sweep`; the csvs, plots, calibration
# tables and provenance beside them stay tracked.
results/conformal_sweep_v1/**/questions/
results/conformal_sweep_v1/**/run.log
results/conformal_sweep_v1/**/demo_bank.jsonl

# Same for v2 (post negative-evidence-migration sweep, three budgets).
results/conformal_sweep_v2/**/questions/
results/oracle_program_posterior/**/questions/
results/voi_policies/**/questions/
results/delayed_label_conformal/**/questions/
# Room-change travel cost: 839 MB of per-question dumps, regenerable
# by `python -m baselines.room_change_cost_study --stage grid`; the
# csvs, figures and findings beside them stay tracked.
results/room_change_cost/**/questions/
results/conformal_sweep_v2/**/run.log
results/conformal_sweep_v2/**/demo_bank.jsonl
results/oracle_lookahead/questions.jsonl

# --- the self-improve study's run outputs, ignored WHERE THEY LIE ------------
# One cell is four streams - searches, looks, notes, call times - and there are several
# hundred cells: about 12 GB, all of it regenerable from the runner, the banks and the
# response cache. `rebuild_the_artifact.py` and everything under `results/self_improve/paper`
# read these in place, so they must not be moved into archive/; they are ignored instead.
# What stays in git is the small stuff beside them, and that is the point of listing the
# outputs by shape rather than by wave: the launchers, the gate output, the compliance
# reports, the VERDICT and CLAIMS files and every queue's pids.txt keep showing up in
# `git status` when they change.
results/**/cells/
results/self_improve/search_driven/hh_s*/
results/self_improve/search_driven/*/hh_s*/
results/self_improve/search_driven/frozen/
results/self_improve/*/logs/**/*.log
results/self_improve/*/logs/*.log
results/self_improve/*/heartbeats.jsonl
results/self_improve/*/*/heartbeats.jsonl

# The generated households: the simulator's day-by-day dumps and the question banks built
# from them. Every one is reproducible from the config.yaml and the seed that sit beside it,
# and both of those ARE in git.
# The simulator's day-by-day dumps for this study's scenarios, named one by one rather than
# by their parent. `data/situation_sim/` shadowed confshift/, which belongs to another study
# and is in git; `data/situation_sim/self_improve/` shadowed illness_v1/, which is in git too.
# A scenario generated in future shows up as untracked, which is the right prompt to decide.
data/situation_sim/self_improve/varied_v1/
data/situation_sim/self_improve/varied_v2/
data/situation_sim/self_improve/guest_v1/
data/situation_sim/self_improve/night_shift_v1/
data/situation_sim/self_improve/room_shut_v1/
data/situation_sim/self_improve/wfh_v1/
results/self_improve/varied_homes/**/sims/
results/self_improve/varied_homes/**/banks/
results/self_improve/runs/*/banks/
results/self_improve/*/*/logs/
# The three small published-method runs have the shape <run>/<variant>/<home>/<arm>/<format>,
# so the `cells/` rule above misses them. Same principle: the streams stay out, the writing
# about them stays in.
results/self_improve/ace_as_published/*/
results/self_improve/memgpt_as_published/*/
results/self_improve/told_if_right/*/
# Derived dumps a script regenerates. Their readable summaries - COMPLIANCE.txt,
# OUTCOMES_*.txt, accuracy_by_day.csv - are in git beside them.
results/self_improve/**/compliance.json
results/self_improve/**/outcomes_*.json
results/self_improve/**/accuracy_by_day.jsonl

# ---- moved out of the tree on 2026-09-29, see ARCHIVE_MANIFEST.md ----
# These are finished or superseded strands. They live at
# /home/oliver/robot/dynamic_home_eqa_archive/ under the same relative paths.
# The rules are here so a script that recreates one does not silently re-add 60,000 files.
results/confidence_shift_2026-09-20/
results/llm_hypotheses/
results/overnight_2026-09-20/
results/sota_memory/
results/regime_search/
preTravelFix/
scratch_runs/
old_profiles/
data/objects/external_props/
data/objects/external_props_candidates/
results/self_improve/archive/

# Type-checker cache: regenerable, and it was 15,271 files.
.mypy_cache/
